    "q-159776": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159776",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(Y\\le 10).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+4\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =2{\\mu }_{1}+4{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =2(-2)+4(3) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=2,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={2}^{2}(2)+{4}^{2}(4)+2(2)(4)(-1) \\\\  & =8+64-16 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(8,56).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\le 10).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 10) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{10-8}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.27)=0.6064.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 10)=0.6064.\n$$",
